it's great to be joined today by Cody  Rosenfield who's an advocate and  independent analyst who's been  investigating oil companies that  manipulate gasoline and oil prices he's  currently working with the Coalition for  clean air a Cody it's great to have you  let's start sort of basic I mean I think  that a lot of people generally have this  understanding that as the price of oil  herbs and flows on some sort of a delay  the price of gasoline that we put in our  cars sort of follows that a band flow is  that true is it that simple how are the  prices of oil and gas determined well  thank you for having me that's the right  question to start with  gasoline prices are absolutely tied to  oil prices but what what I end up seeing  is places with market power problems  places where there are too few oil  companies with too much power in the  market that's where it starts to get a  little strange so there will be an  increase in oil price but the gasoline  price will jump exceptionally more or  there'll or for example oil prices will  drop and there will be no impact on the  gasoline price so it actually kind of  depends on which market you're looking  at but there are many markets in the  United States where gasoline prices are  almost completely divorced from the oil  prices that they're supposed to be  related to to get this part out of the  way first and we'll sort of work up the  chain does the or how much does the  local gas station decide in terms of the  gasoline price is it on the matter of a  couple of cents and basically they've  got a small markup on a market that they  really don't have any influence on or as  you know how they're there are certainly  gas stations that are known as always  the most expensive or always the  cheapest but how much of that  variability is really being determined  by the gas station well actually very  little of the station owner has very  little power to set a price when when  you're a gas station owner especially  under a branded contract so if you if  you own a exxon mode you know a Mobil  station or a BP station you have very  little ability to choose what your price  is you're given a cost that you're gonna  pay the oil company to receive gasoline  and you have to stay within a very small  boundary of what  is you're allowed to charge or else the  company that's providing you the  gasoline will actually start to punish  you and charge you higher prices so most  of the actual pricing problems don't  come from the stations they come from  the companies selling to the stations  and an important part of this to  remember is that the prices that you see  that US consumers pay those are almost  completely unregulated a gas station  across the street from you could decide  to choose to charge $12 per gallon it  wouldn't work very well but they could  do it  where the regulations actually kick in  are on the wholesale markets and the  sales from those oil companies to the  gas stations good so let's now dig into  the different factors that allow the oil  companies to manipulate the prices that  they charge and are ultimately paid yeah  absolutely there's a number of ways that  it happens and it like I said earlier it  starts mostly with a market power  problem so for example in California we  have more oil companies that control  about 80% of the gasoline in the state  and California is not a market to sneeze  at if you look at gasoline markets of  the world California is the third  largest gasoline market after the United  States as a whole and then China so when  we're talking about small increases in  gas prices in a place like California  we're talking about essentially the  largest regional gasoline market in the  world and billions of dollars per month  that are being accrued by these oil  companies so it starts usually with  there being too few companies and then  from there there are a number of tactics  they can use from manipulating refinery  schedules when they're going online and  offline all the way to ships being  parked offshore and intentionally  confusing traders in that market to  cause the price either to raise or lower  yet the ships parked offshore to me was  reminiscent of what's going on in the  diamond industry and I've my audience  knows I am significantly opposed to the  entire practices of the diamond industry  and this idea that at least part of why  there's an argument to be made for the  high cost of diamonds is limited supply  but that's an hour  officially limited supply that we know  we know there are significant amounts of  diamonds that are just kept sort of  outside of the known or at least  quantifiable supply that am I wrong that  that's very similar to what's going on  with oil no it's absolutely similar  there's a there's a monopoly in the  diamond industry and that's why diamonds  are worth anything more than another  stone but with oil oil is similar in  that it also very naturally draws market  power and monopoly problems it's not a  coincidence that through the history of  oil there's always been some giant  player trying to change prices whether  it was Standard Oil or whether it's OPEC  today so just like diamonds  it's a these types of commodities if you  control the infrastructure you can  essentially it's very hard to stop  certain companies from getting too much  control and being able to essentially  set prices that's a very good example  with the diamond industry so as far as  it let's take an industry like the  airline industry right that really is is  directly impacted by the cost of jet  fuel which of course is derived from oil  there's sometimes the perception from  the air traveler that as the price of  oil and jet fuel goes up the fuel  surcharge on tickets seems to go up in  proportion or maybe even above and  beyond what one might imagine it should  but then you never hear about oh the  cost of the tickets is now coming down  because oil prices are down right sort  of the idea that as the costs go up the  price of a ticket goes up but then it  never comes back down when the cost of  oil goes down is that perception  accurate or is it just that it doesn't  make headlines when the fuel surcharges  are coming down it's probably the truth  is probably in the middle of there there  is always in commodity markets a concept  of rising like a rocket and dropping  like a feather right so you you see a  little problem on an oil market and  suddenly the price explodes but it takes  seven months for the price to lower back  to where it was when it comes to the jet  fuel and is obviously jet fuel has a lot  of similar problems to the gasoline and  oil manipulation as far as how exactly  it connects to prices for for flying  done that exact research but there is  definitely as you said a correlation  between it going up but not going down  when prices change so talk a little bit  about regulation around the manipulation  of prices I mean certainly the oil  companies would say no listen what what  people like Cody accused us of doing we  can't really do because of this  regulation and that regulation but is  there anything to that through that or  are those regulations basically designed  in a way where they can be circumvented  well actually we we have a number of  extremely strong regulations the problem  is there has been almost no interest by  federal or state regulators to do to  actually utilize the strong regulations  we have so in 2008 when gasoline prices  exploded to near $5 and some parts of  the country Obama President Obama  created a task force specifically to  look at gasoline prices and they were  never heard from again after the press  conference not that anything happened to  them but they never did another meeting  after the initial press conference and  actually the Federal Trade Commission  has regulations that are extremely  strong regarding energy regarding  wholesale trades for gasoline and oil  but the problem is they've actually  never even utilized on their own rule  that they invented so while there are  strong regulations I've always found  that the problem is regulators either  are too intimidated to actually use them  or there's such a barrier to beginning  to do this investigative work because  it's so expensive behind the data or  they don't know the industry well enough  that that really keeps the regulator's  from being from getting involved with  the election of President Trump this is  another huge problem because federal  regulators are the ones that should be  really looking at these these markets  but they're obviously completely  uninterested in doing so so that's when  the it kind of turns towards state  regulators and if you any Attorney  General in the United States any City  Attorney any district attorney or US  attorney could essentially try one of  these cases because these are very basic  commodity laws being violated it's  mostly just trying to actually  get a politician or an elected or you  know an appointed official could be  interested in doing the work  yeah really no political will to do it  is what it seems like Cody if people  have leads for you what's the best way  for people to get in touch well they can  reach out to me a number of ways that  there's a small network of people who  work on this investigative issue from  all across the world and the way to get  involved would be to email oil price  investigation at gmail.com would be the  best place for them to go we've been  speaking with Cody Rosenfield he's an  advocate and independent analyst who's  been investigating oil companies that  manipulate gas and oil prices currently  working with the Coalition for clean air  Cody thanks a lot for talking to us  about this yeah of course thank you for  having me

